offlinecompiler: code written at runtime is not compiled from the file.

A code map records where a guest block ran, not what it was. The runtime
translated it from memory; the offline compiler translates the same address
from the image mapped off disk. For a packed image those are different bytes:
a code section with SizeOfRawData == 0 is zeroes on disk and filled by a stub
at startup, and a writable code section can be rewritten in place.

Measured: one packed steam_api64.dll (WUS0 raw size 0, virtual 0x1EF000, RWX)
shared byte-for-byte by four games in one container. The first launch of each
container had no cache and ran. Every launch after it loaded a translation
compiled from the empty section: EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION at +0x17F0FE,
the DLL's handler faulting on a null read, c0000409, 1,223 nested dispatches,
8 MB main stack exhausted.

Blocks whose first byte is outside file data, or inside a writable section, are
now left out of the cache and translated at runtime as on the first launch.
Ordinary images -- read-execute code sections with file data behind every
byte -- produce the same cache as before.

--- a/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
@@ -474,6 +474,69 @@
 #endif
 
 // Returns filename of generated cache on success
+#ifdef _WIN32
+/* Vessel: a block may be compiled ahead of time only if its bytes are the
+ * file's bytes.
+ *
+ * A code map records *where* a guest block ran, not what it was. The runtime
+ * translated it from memory; this tool translates the same address from the
+ * image mapped off disk. Those are the same bytes for an ordinary code section
+ * and different bytes for two kinds of image:
+ *
+ *  - **A section with no file data.** A packer declares its real code section
+ *    with `SizeOfRawData == 0` and a large `VirtualSize`, and a stub writes the
+ *    code into it at startup. Mapped off disk that section is zeroes, so every
+ *    block recorded inside it is compiled from zeroes.
+ *  - **A writable code section.** Code that can be written can be rewritten --
+ *    decrypted in place, patched by the stub -- so even bytes the file does
+ *    carry are not the bytes that ran.
+ *
+ * Measured on a Steam API DLL packed exactly this way (`WUS0`: raw size 0,
+ * virtual size 0x1EF000, RWX; `WUS1`: RWX, the unpacker). The first launch had
+ * no cache, translated the unpacked code from memory and ran. This tool then
+ * compiled the recorded blocks from the empty section, and **every launch after
+ * the first** loaded that translation instead: `EXCEPTION_ILLEGAL_INSTRUCTION`
+ * at `+0x17F0FE`, the DLL's own handler faulting on a null read, `c0000409`,
+ * and 1,223 nested dispatches until the 8 MB main stack was gone. "Works once
+ * on a fresh container, never again" is this, and a user cannot tell it from
+ * the game being broken.
+ *
+ * Skipping costs nothing that was ever real: those blocks are translated at
+ * runtime, from memory, which is what worked. An ordinary image's code
+ * sections are read-execute with file data behind every byte, so its cache is
+ * unchanged. The runtime side needs no matching change -- the cache simply
+ * does not contain these blocks -- and the invalidation tracker still sees the
+ * writes at runtime as it always did.
+ *
+ * Judged on the block's first byte. A block starting in file data and running
+ * off the end of it would need a decoder here to find; a code section whose
+ * raw size ends mid-function is not a layout any linker produces, and a packer
+ * that does it is caught by the writable test anyway. */
+static bool BlockIsFromFile(uintptr_t ImageBase, uint64_t Rva) {
+  const auto* NtHeaders = reinterpret_cast<const FEX::Windows::ArchImageNtHeaders*>(RtlImageNtHeader(reinterpret_cast<HMODULE>(ImageBase)));
+  if (!NtHeaders) {
+    return false;
+  }
+
+  const auto* Section = IMAGE_FIRST_SECTION(NtHeaders);
+  for (WORD i = 0; i < NtHeaders->FileHeader.NumberOfSections; ++i, ++Section) {
+    const uint64_t Start = Section->VirtualAddress;
+    const uint64_t Span = std::max<uint64_t>(Section->Misc.VirtualSize, Section->SizeOfRawData);
+    if (Rva < Start || Rva >= Start + Span) {
+      continue;
+    }
+    if (Section->Characteristics & IMAGE_SCN_MEM_WRITE) {
+      return false;
+    }
+    return Rva - Start < Section->SizeOfRawData;
+  }
+
+  // Outside every section: the headers, or past the image. Neither is code a
+  // cache should carry.
+  return false;
+}
+#endif
+
